Native signal fft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names.

Computes the one-dimensional discrete Fourier Transform.

This function uses the `rustfft` library to perform the FFT.

# Arguments
* `input` - A slice of complex samples. Its contents are preserved.

# Returns
A vector of complex numbers representing the FFT of the input.

# Example
```rust
use rssn::kernels::signal::fft;
use rustfft::num_complex::Complex;

let mut input = vec![
    Complex::new(1.0, 0.0),
    Complex::new(1.0, 0.0),
    Complex::new(1.0, 0.0),
    Complex::new(1.0, 0.0),
];

let output = fft(&mut input);

assert!((output[0].re - 4.0).abs() < 1e-9);
```

Native signal ifft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names.

Computes the one-dimensional inverse discrete Fourier Transform.

This is the positive-sign transform followed by the `1/n`
normalisation, so `ifft(fft(x)) == x` (up to round-off) for every length.

# Arguments
* `input` - A mutable slice of complex frequency-domain samples.

# Returns
A vector of complex numbers representing the inverse transform.

# Example
```rust
use rssn::kernels::signal::fft;
use rssn::kernels::signal::ifft;
use rustfft::num_complex::Complex;

let mut input = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 0.5), Complex::new(-2.0, 0.0)];

let spectrum = fft(&mut input);

let back = ifft(&mut spectrum.clone());

assert!((back[0] - input[0]).norm() < 1e-12);
```

Native transforms fft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names.

Computes the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) of a sequence of complex numbers.

Works at any length and leaves the length unchanged using a shared,
runtime-selected SIMD FFT plan from [`super::fft`].

# Arguments
* `data` - A mutable slice of `Complex<f64>` representing the input sequence.

# Example
```rust
use num_complex::Complex;
use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft;

let mut data = vec![
    Complex::new(1.0, 0.0),
    Complex::new(1.0, 0.0),
    Complex::new(0.0, 0.0),
    Complex::new(0.0, 0.0),
];

fft(&mut data);

assert!((data[0].re - 2.0).abs() < 1e-9);
```

A length that is not a power of two keeps its length:
```rust
use num_complex::Complex;
use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft;

let mut data = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 0.0); 3];

fft(&mut data);

assert_eq!(data.len(), 3);
// [3, 0, 0] for a constant signal.
assert!((data[0].re - 3.0).abs() < 1e-12);
```

Native transforms ifft; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names.

Computes the Inverse Fast Fourier Transform (IFFT).

Works at any length and leaves the length unchanged, so `ifft(fft(x))`
returns `x` at every length. The `1/n` normalisation is included.

# Arguments
* `data` - A mutable `Vec<Complex<f64>>` representing the input frequency-domain sequence.

# Example
```rust
use num_complex::Complex;
use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft;
use rssn::kernels::transforms::ifft;

let mut data = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 2.0), Complex::new(3.0, 4.0)];

let original = data.clone();

fft(&mut data);

ifft(&mut data);

assert!((data[0].re - original[0].re).abs() < 1e-9);

assert!((data[0].im - original[0].im).abs() < 1e-9);
```

Native transforms ifft_slice; normalized dimensionless arguments unless the declared contract preserves measured signal units. Enum arguments use exact Rust variant names.

Computes the Inverse FFT for a slice.

**Use this for efficient, parallel chunk processing (e.g., with rayon).**
Works at any length and operates in place; the `1/n` normalisation is
included. Bit-identical to [`ifft`].

# Arguments
* `data` - A mutable slice of `Complex<f64>` representing the input frequency-domain sequence.

# Example
```rust
use num_complex::Complex;
use rssn::kernels::transforms::fft_slice;
use rssn::kernels::transforms::ifft_slice;

let mut data = vec![Complex::new(1.0, 0.0), Complex::new(0.0, 0.0)];

let original = data.clone();

fft_slice(&mut data);

ifft_slice(&mut data);

assert!((data[0].re - original[0].re).abs() < 1e-9);
```

Computes the CRC-16 checksum of the given data.

Uses the IBM/ANSI polynomial (0xA001 in reflected form).

# Arguments
* `data` - The data to compute the checksum for.

# Returns
The 16-bit CRC checksum.

# Example
```
use rssn::kernels::error_correction::crc16_compute;

let data = b"123456789";

let checksum = crc16_compute(data);

assert_eq!(checksum, 0xBB3D);
```

Computes the CRC-32 checksum of the given data.

Uses the standard IEEE 802.3 polynomial (0xEDB88320 in reflected form).

# Arguments
* `data` - The data to compute the checksum for.

# Returns
The 32-bit CRC checksum.

# Example
```
use rssn::kernels::error_correction::crc32_compute_numerical;

let data = b"Hello, World!";

let checksum = crc32_compute_numerical(data);

assert_eq!(checksum, 0xEC4AC3D0);
```

Decodes a 7-bit Hamming(7,4) codeword, correcting a single-bit error if found.

# Arguments
* `codeword` - A slice of 7 bytes, each representing a bit (0 or 1).

# Returns
A `Result` containing:
- `Ok((data, error_pos))` where `data` is the 4-bit corrected data and `error_pos` is
  `Some(index)` if an error was corrected at the given 1-based index, or `None` if no error was found.
- `Err(String)` if the input length is not 7.

# Errors
Returns an error if the input codeword length is not exactly 7.

# Example
```
use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_decode_numerical;
use rssn::kernels::error_correction::hamming_encode_numerical;

let data = vec![1, 0, 1, 1];

let mut codeword = hamming_encode_numerical(&data).unwrap();

codeword[2] ^= 1; // Introduce error at position 3 (1-indexed)
let (decoded, error_pos) = hamming_decode_numerical(&codeword).unwrap();

assert_eq!(decoded, data);

assert_eq!(error_pos, Some(3));
```

Native staggered acoustic call in a mechanical reference frame. In dimension d, density is Mass/Length^d and pressure is Energy/Length^d, representing the explicitly reduced continuum. Velocity is Length/Time and total energy is Energy. All numeric coordinates, spacings, speed, density and arrays are normalized by these references. Compiled physical callbacks retain dimensions and finite evaluation budgets; failed mutations invalidate their solver handle.

Initialises `v^{-1/2}` from `p^0` for a start from rest (velocity zero at `t = 0`);
call once after [`Acoustics::set_pressure`]. Not for PML runs.

Native boundary model. Scalar/fluid quantities use the explicit grid reference frame; standalone elasticity boundary coefficients are normalized and require the solver material/reference frame. Marker and capability bounds are checked.

Constant normal-pressure traction `σ n = -p n` is built by the caller from `n`;
this helper fixes only one displacement component to zero (a roller).

Native interpolation over normalized samples with explicit coordinate and ordinate references. Physical queries and outputs are checked against retained domains; point/order/allocation bounds are enforced.

Creates a cubic spline interpolator for a given set of points.

Cubic spline interpolation constructs a piecewise cubic polynomial that passes
through each data point, ensuring smoothness (continuity of first and second derivatives)
at the interior points. The input points must be sorted by their x-coordinates.

# Arguments
* `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples. Must be sorted by x.

# Returns
A `Result` containing a closure `Arc<dyn Fn(f64) -> f64>` that can be used to evaluate
the spline at any point.

# Errors
Returns an error if fewer than two points are provided, or if the x-coordinates are not strictly increasing.

# Example
```rust
use rssn::kernels::interpolate::cubic_spline_interpolation;

let points = vec![(0.0, 0.0), (1.0, 1.0), (2.0, 0.0)];

let spline = cubic_spline_interpolation(&points).unwrap();

// Check that interpolated value is reasonable (between the y values)
let val = spline(0.5);

assert!((spline(0.5) - 0.6875).abs() < 1e-9);
assert!(val > 0.0 && val < 1.0);
```

Native interpolation over normalized samples with explicit coordinate and ordinate references. Physical queries and outputs are checked against retained domains; point/order/allocation bounds are enforced.

Monotone piecewise cubic Hermite interpolation (PCHIP, Fritsch-Carlson).

The interpolant is `C^1` and, unlike a plain cubic spline, preserves the shape of the
data: on every interval its values stay inside the range of the two end ordinates, so
monotone data stay monotone and a step is not overshot. Third order in the node
spacing where the slope has one sign, second order at a data extremum, where the
slope estimate is clipped to zero — the price of shape preservation. Values outside
the node range are extrapolated with the first or the last Hermite cubic.

# Arguments
* `points` - A slice of `(x, y)` tuples with strictly increasing `x`.

# Returns
A `Result` containing a closure `Arc<dyn Fn(f64) -> f64>` evaluating the interpolant.

# Errors
Returns an error if fewer than two points are given or the abscissae are not strictly
increasing.

# Example
```rust
use rssn::kernels::interpolate::pchip_interpolation;

// A step: the monotone interpolant stays within [0, 1].
let step = [(0.0, 0.0), (1.0, 0.0), (2.0, 1.0), (3.0, 1.0)];

let f = pchip_interpolation(&step).unwrap();

for k in 0..=30 {
    let v = f(f64::from(k) / 10.0);

    assert!((-1e-12..=1.0 + 1e-12).contains(&v));
}
```

Computes the covariant Ricci tensor and scalar curvature with one finite-difference metric stencil and factorization. Coordinates use a common dimension in one to six dimensions. With coordinate dimension X and metric dimension G, tensor entries have X^-2 and the scalar has X^-2/G. Use positive coordinate and metric references and supply a callback evaluation budget.

Computes the Ricci tensor and scalar with one curvature stencil and one
factorization of the metric at `point`.

```
use rssn::kernels::differential_geometry::ricci_curvature;
let flat = |_: &[f64]| vec![vec![1.0, 0.0], vec![0.0, 1.0]];
let curvature = ricci_curvature(flat, &[0.0, 0.0])?;
assert_eq!(curvature.scalar, 0.0);
```

# Errors

Returns an error for an invalid or singular metric at a connection stencil
point, or an unrepresentable stencil or result.

